<p>After a hailstorm, a burst pipe, or a fire, every week without an answer has a cost. The tarp is still on the roof, the drywall is cut open, and you are paying for temporary fixes while you wait. A <strong>delayed insurance claim in Dallas</strong> can stall for ordinary reasons, like a surge of claims after a major storm, or for frustrating ones, like repeated document requests, adjusters who change every few weeks, or an estimate stuck in review. Texas law sets general timelines for how insurers handle claims. Knowing those timelines and documenting every step can help you push a slow claim forward.</p>

<h2>Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Timelines</h2>
<p>The Texas Prompt Payment of Claims statute, found in <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542, Subchapter B</a>, sets general deadlines for most property insurers. In broad terms:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>General timeline</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin investigating, and request information</td><td>Within 15 days after notice of the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject the claim</td><td>Within 15 business days after receiving all requested items</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extension if more time is needed</td><td>Insurer must notify you; up to 45 additional days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay after accepting</td><td>Within 5 business days after notifying you of acceptance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Declared weather catastrophe</td><td>Deadlines generally extended by 15 days</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Eligible surplus lines insurers have different timeframes (generally 30 business days), and the details of your situation matter. Use these timelines as a guide for asking good questions, not as legal advice.</p>

<h2>Why Claims Get Delayed in Dallas</h2>
<h3>Catastrophe-level claim volume</h3>
<p>Dallas County’s hail season runs roughly March through June and peaks in April and May, with another bump in October. When a large storm hits a dense area, such as the hail and tornadoes of April 3, 2012 or the May 28, 2024 windstorm that brought winds up to 95 mph in Garland, insurers bring in outside adjusters and inspections can be backed up for weeks.</p>
<h3>Freeze events across the whole region</h3>
<p>Winter Storm Uri in February 2021 produced burst pipes across North Texas within a few days, overwhelming plumbers, mitigation companies, and adjusters at the same time. See <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a>.</p>
<h3>Open-ended document requests</h3>
<p>Repeated requests for receipts, photos, recorded statements, or proof of loss can keep a claim in limbo. The statutory decision clock generally runs from when the insurer receives the items it asked for, so clear records of what was requested and when you delivered it matter.</p>
<h3>Adjuster turnover and reviews</h3>
<p>Files that pass between field adjusters, desk adjusters, and supervisors can lose momentum. Engineering reports and large-loss reviews can add more time.</p>

